 from  philadelphia great pleasure to have you  with us  sir just from the outset it must have  just been a relief  for all the other nato members to see  joe biden turn up i mean okay obviously  they knew he was coming but they just  know that it's going to be a it's going  to be a smoother ride  yeah that's certainly the case uh we  have a president who really believes in  nato  who speaks in a polite and friendly way  to his fellow allies doesn't bully them  so  uh breath of fresh air from the allied  point of view has it been a good nato  summit for  for joe budden i was saying to a  correspondent earlier how he wanted  china front and center and that has been  the biggest thing to come out of the the  communique  yes i agree uh i think he got more than  he even may have expected from this nato  summit  because there are some differences at  least a difference of emphasis among  allies on  whether china is really a threat or  whether it's a more manageable  challenge compared to russia but he did  get some strong language  on china on the systemic challenge that  it poses to the rules-based  international order  and he did get agreement to pursue an  agenda that's very forward-looking  which in its different components will  also deal with  with those challenges from china whether  it's new technologies  nuclear buildup of cyber attacks  uh the agenda for nato 2030 as was  adopted at this uh summit  is uh directed against everybody but it  has a lot of relevance for the china  challenge  it's their the possibility though the  risk even of getting too focused on  china  and russia for that matter i know they  are perceived as the biggest threats for  nato but there's a whole lot of other  stuff  and other issues within the nato family  which need to be sorted out  oh yes has more than its fair share of  challenges  including some internal ones but i think  that uh  on russia the language is strong but  it's  appropriately strong because russia has  been really a 